NAME
       init - first process, service manager

SYNOPSIS
       /sbin/init

DESCRIPTION
       The kernel starts init as PID 1. Like systemd, init reads unit files,
       starts the default target with everything it wants in dependency order,
       restarts services that die and reaps every orphaned process. It is
       controlled with initctl(8) (also installed as systemctl).

UNIT FILES
       Searched in /etc/init/system (administrator), /run/init/system and
       /usr/lib/init/system (packages); the first found wins. An empty file
       masks a unit. NAME@.service is a template: getty@tty2.service uses
       getty@.service with %i (and %I) replaced by tty2, %n by the unit name.

           [Unit]
           Description=Network Daemon
           Requires=sysinit.target       started first, failure stops us
           Wants=other.service           started too, failure is ignored
           After=sysinit.target          wait until these are up
           Before=...                    the reverse of After
           Conflicts=rescue.target       stopped when we start
           ConditionPathExists=[!]/path  skip silently when not met

           [Service]
           Type=simple                   or oneshot (runs to the end)
           ExecStartPre=, ExecStart=, ExecStartPost=, ExecStop=
                                         full path; a leading - ignores failure
           Restart=no                    always, on-failure
           RestartSec=1                  seconds (or 500ms, 2min)
           RemainAfterExit=no            oneshot stays active
           User=name                     run as this user
           WorkingDirectory=/path
           Environment=KEY=value         may repeat
           StandardOutput=log            log (/var/log/init/NAME.log), null,
                                         tty, tty:/dev/ttyN, file:PATH, append:PATH

           [Install]
           WantedBy=multi-user.target    used by initctl enable

       A target groups units: it waits for everything it wants. Units are
       enabled by a file named after the unit in TARGET.wants/ (or
       TARGET.requires/) in a unit directory; initctl enable creates it.

       A service that dies 5 times within 10 seconds is paused for 10
       seconds. On poweroff and reboot services get SIGTERM, then SIGKILL
       after 5 seconds.

TARGETS
       sysinit.target, multi-user.target (consoles, network), graphical.target
       (multi-user plus display-manager.service), rescue.target (only tty1).

KERNEL COMMAND LINE
       init.unit=NAME.target
           start this target instead of the default
       text, 3, nogui
           multi-user.target
       single, 1, rescue
           rescue.target
       verbose, loglevel=7
           keep printing all kernel messages on tty1

       Without anything the target is read from /etc/init/default.target,
       otherwise multi-user.target. When no unit can be loaded init runs getty
       on tty1-tty6.

FILES
       /etc/init/system/, /usr/lib/init/system/, /etc/init/default.target,
       /run/init/units (state), /run/init/req/ (requests), /var/log/init/

SEE ALSO
       initctl(8), getty(8), netd(8), chvt(1)
